2026-11-22 11:51:09 - Agent_20261122115044 - INFO - [+] Agent signaled completion: Reviewed both CodeQL results, which both point at the same construct: src/dotenv/cli.py line 213 (`Popen(command, universal_newlines=True, bufsize=0, shell=False, env=cmd_env)`) inside `run_command()`, plus the adjacent `os.execvpe(command[0], args=command, env=cmd_env)` at line 218.

Verdict: false positives for CWE-78. The `command` argument originates from the `dotenv run <cmd...>` CLI invocation (`click.argument("commandline", nargs=-1, type=click.UNPROCESSED)`), i.e. arguments deliberately typed by the operator in their own shell — the documented, intended behavior of the subcommand (equivalent to `env`/`dotenvx run`). Crucially, the call explicitly passes `shell=False`, and `os.execvpe` on POSIX execs the binary directly with an argv list; neither path performs shell metacharacter interpretation, so no command-injection sink exists. I found no attacker-controlled data (e.g. .env file contents) flowing into the executable or its arguments — the .env values only populate the child's *environment* via `cmd_env`.

Additional sweeps found no other execution sinks in the package: no `shell=True`, no `os.system`/`os.popen`/`os.exec*` with string interpolation, and no dynamic-eval patterns (`eval`/`exec`/`pickle`/`yaml.load`). Note `shlex.quote()` is correctly applied for the `export`/`shell` list formats, and `dotenv set` uses `set_key` rather than string-built shell commands.

No new vulnerabilities discovered. Submitted 2 findings, both `fp`.
